From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: v-lix <280294695+v-lix@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/dca_xll: recognise the remaining DTS:X extension
 syncwords, and report which

The DTS:X extension is named by the syncword at the dword-aligned end of the
XLL band data. Upstream tests two values, and 0005 split the second so that
0xF14000D1 reports plain DTS:X while only 0xF14000D0 reports DTS:X IMAX. Three
further values - 0xF14000D2, 0xF14000D3 and 0xF14000D4 - match neither test,
so a stream carrying any of them falls through and is reported as ordinary
DTS-HD MA. Kodi builds its label from that profile, so those streams arrive
with no DTS:X label at all.

MediaInfoLib already sorts the whole family the way this patch does. Its
File_Dts::Extensions2() sets presence_Extended_X for 0x02000850 and for
0xF14000D1 through 0xF14000D4, and only 0xF14000D0 additionally sets
presence_Extended_IMAX. That list, D2 included, was written by the same author
whose earlier reverse engineering 0005 rests on:

  MediaArea/MediaInfoLib#2653, which adds
    case 0x02000850: case 0xF14000D1: case 0xF14000D2:
    case 0xF14000D3: case 0xF14000D4:
        Presence.set(presence_Extended_X);
  https://github.com/MediaArea/MediaInfoLib/blob/master/Source/MediaInfo/Audio/File_Dts.cpp
  https://github.com/MediaArea/MediaInfo/issues/717
  https://github.com/MediaArea/MediaInfo/issues/823

This patch takes the three it treats as plain DTS:X that 0005 did not reach,
and puts them in the branch 0005 put 0xF14000D1 in.

0xF14000D4 was confirmed against DTS:X Pro samples besides: it appears exactly
once per XLL frame, dword-aligned where this code already looks, and the
CRC-protected envelope behind it decodes as five objects over the 7.1 bed and
four fixed heights. Built against ffmpeg 8.1.2 with 0005 and this patch
applied in order, ffprobe reports those samples as "DTS-HD MA + DTS:X" where
it reported "DTS-HD MA" before. No sample was available for 0xF14000D2 or
0xF14000D3; they rest on the classification above, as 0xF14000D1 did.

Like the rest of the DTS:X detection here, this is reverse engineering rather
than specification, and carries the same false-positive risk as the syncwords
already listed.

Reporting which one is the other half. Collapsing all five into one boolean
throws away the only thing in the stream that says how many objects it
declares. The syncword is not an opaque marker: 0xF14000Dn is the first four
bytes of the type-241 object metadata element, and after that element's 28-bit
fixed header the next four bits are its declaration count minus one - which is
the low nibble of the last byte. So the marker states the count by construction
rather than by correlation: D0 one object, D1 two, D2 three, D3 four, D4 five.

Two IMAX Enhanced streams confirm the D0 end of it. Both parse with a
declaration count of one: the five-feed form carries that object alone, and the
seven-feed form carries it as three contributions to the compatible bed's C, L
and R. The older 0x02000850 form has no such nibble and declares no objects.

The nibble is reported in the level, and the profile is left exactly as it was.
A profile per object count would mint a codec identity for what is a property
of one: DTS:X with two objects and DTS:X with five are the same codec, and
every consumer that enumerates the DTS-HD MA family by hand - passthrough
routing, stream naming, codec selection - would have to learn each new value or
quietly mishandle the stream. The level costs none of that. Nothing in the DCA
decoder writes it, avcodec_parameters_from_context() already copies it beside
the profile, and it arrives wherever the profile arrives, so an existing caller
needs no new plumbing and an uninterested one sees no change at all.

It is passed on as transmitted, the nibble rather than a count, so that the
decoder reports what the stream said and the reading of it stays with whoever
asked. Only the 0xF14000Dn family is recorded for it: 0x02000850 is not an
object metadata element and its last byte is not a count, so a stream carrying
that form leaves the level at the AV_LEVEL_UNKNOWN it started at. That is how a
stream which said nothing about objects is told from D0's declaration of one,
and it is very nearly every DTS:X release in the wild.

The syncword's lifetime is the decoder context's, and matching the two present
flags beside it is the point. Those are set once and never cleared, so the
profile describes every frame the context has seen rather than the frame in
hand; the level now says the same thing about the same frames. Clearing it on a
flush instead would empty the count on a seek while the profile went on saying
DTS:X. The cost of the parity is a context that reads D0 through D4 and then
the older form, which keeps the last count it was given - and keeps saying
DTS:X for it, on the same terms, as it already did before this patch.

The count is not the whole shape of the presentation, though. Every form seen
puts the four heights over its bed but one: D0 also marks a 5.1 variant with a
single object and no heights at all, which a declaration count of one cannot
tell from the 7.1 D0 form beside it. The element says which itself. Its
payload opens with a CRC-protected prefix that ends in a byte naming the
channel sets that follow - bit 0 the object set, always present, bit 1 the
height quartet - and then the constant tail 34 38 8c 4f 00. So 03 is objects
and heights, and 01 objects alone.

ff_dca_x_height_flags() reads that byte the way harletty-bridge, whose decoder
established the reading and renders both variants, finds it: 16 to 96 bytes
in, followed by the tail, with a zero CRC16-CCITT remainder over everything
before it, and a second candidate taken as no answer rather than a guess. It
costs one CRC over at most 96 bytes of a frame already in memory, and decodes
nothing. It lives in dca.c, which the decoder and the parser both link, so the
HRA patch can use it as well.

What it finds goes above the nibble, in the same level: DCA_X_LEVEL_SETS_READ
(0x10) says the byte was read, and DCA_X_LEVEL_HEIGHTS (0x20) that the quartet
follows. With the first clear nothing was read and nothing is said, so a caller
can tell "no heights" from "not known" and leave the heights out rather than
invent them. The flags last as long as the syncword, for the same reason, and a
frame whose byte cannot be read keeps what an earlier one said. The 0x02000850
form is no such element and is left exactly as it was.

The same field is where a later patch can report which layout an Auro-3D
carrier unfolds to, for the same reason and without disturbing anything here:
the profile says which codec, the level says which variant of it.

Applied in order against ffmpeg 8.1.2, 0005, this patch and the Auro-3D patch
all apply with no rejects.
---
 libavcodec/dca.c           | 30 +++++++++++++++++++
 libavcodec/dca.h           | 24 ++++++++++++++++
 libavcodec/dca_syncwords.h |  3 ++
 libavcodec/dca_xll.c       | 59 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 libavcodec/dca_xll.h       |  2 ++
 5 files changed, 117 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/libavcodec/dca.c b/libavcodec/dca.c
--- a/libavcodec/dca.c
+++ b/libavcodec/dca.c
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 #include <stdint.h>
 #include <string.h>
 
+#include "libavutil/crc.h"
 #include "libavutil/error.h"
 
 #include "dca.h"
@@ -155,3 +156,32 @@ int avpriv_dca_parse_core_frame_header(DCACoreFrameHeader *h, const uint8_t *buf
 
     return 0;
 }
+
+int ff_dca_x_height_flags(const uint8_t *payload, int size)
+{
+    static const uint8_t tail[] = { 0x34, 0x38, 0x8c, 0x4f, 0x00 };
+    const AVCRC *crc = av_crc_get_table(AV_CRC_16_CCITT);
+    int found = -1;
+
+    /* The same search harletty-bridge, which established this reading, makes:
+     * the set byte somewhere from 16 to 96 bytes in (the shortest prefix seen
+     * is the object-only form's 20), followed by the tail, with a zero CRC
+     * remainder over everything before it - so a stray tail in coded data is
+     * not enough. A second match leaves the boundary ambiguous, and that is
+     * read as nothing. */
+    for (int pos = 16; pos < FFMIN(size, 96) && pos + 1 + (int)sizeof(tail) <= size; pos++) {
+        int sets = payload[pos];
+
+        if ((sets & ~3) || !(sets & 1) ||
+            memcmp(payload + pos + 1, tail, sizeof(tail)) ||
+            av_crc(crc, 0xffff, payload, pos))
+            continue;
+        if (found >= 0)
+            return 0;
+        found = sets;
+    }
+
+    if (found < 0)
+        return 0;
+    return DCA_X_LEVEL_SETS_READ | (found & 2 ? DCA_X_LEVEL_HEIGHTS : 0);
+}
diff --git a/libavcodec/dca.h b/libavcodec/dca.h
--- a/libavcodec/dca.h
+++ b/libavcodec/dca.h
@@ -223,4 +223,28 @@ int avpriv_dca_parse_core_frame_header(DCACoreFrameHeader *h, const uint8_t *buf
  */
 int ff_dca_parse_core_frame_header(DCACoreFrameHeader *h, GetBitContext *gb);
 
+/**
+ * What an alternate-profile DTS:X payload says about the channel sets it
+ * carries, reported in the level beside the object count.
+ *
+ * The 0xF14000Dn element opens a CRC-protected prefix that ends in a byte
+ * naming the channel sets which follow, then the constant tail
+ * 34 38 8c 4f 00: bit 0 is the object set, always present, and bit 1 the
+ * height quartet. Every form seen carries both but one, 5.1 with a single
+ * object and no heights at all, and the declaration count cannot tell that
+ * form from the 7.1.4 one beside it.
+ */
+#define DCA_X_LEVEL_SETS_READ 0x10 ///< the channel-set byte was read
+#define DCA_X_LEVEL_HEIGHTS   0x20 ///< the height quartet follows
+
+/**
+ * Read the channel-set byte of an alternate-profile DTS:X payload.
+ * @param payload the payload, starting at its 0xF14000Dn syncword
+ * @param size    bytes available from there
+ * @return DCA_X_LEVEL_SETS_READ, with DCA_X_LEVEL_HEIGHTS when the height
+ *         quartet follows; 0 when no single protected prefix is found, which
+ *         says nothing either way
+ */
+int ff_dca_x_height_flags(const uint8_t *payload, int size);
+
 #endif /* AVCODEC_DCA_H */
diff --git a/libavcodec/dca_syncwords.h b/libavcodec/dca_syncwords.h
--- a/libavcodec/dca_syncwords.h
+++ b/libavcodec/dca_syncwords.h
@@ -35,6 +35,9 @@
 
 #define    DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_1              0x02000850U
 #define    DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_2              0xF14000D1U
+#define    DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_3              0xF14000D2U
+#define    DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_4              0xF14000D3U
+#define    DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_5              0xF14000D4U
 #define    DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_IMAX           0xF14000D0U
 
 #endif /* AVCODEC_DCA_SYNCWORDS_H */
diff --git a/libavcodec/dca_xll.c b/libavcodec/dca_xll.c
--- a/libavcodec/dca_xll.c
+++ b/libavcodec/dca_xll.c
@@ -1073,10 +1073,28 @@ static int parse_frame(DCAXllDecoder *s, const uint8_t *data, int size, DCAExssA
 
         extradata_syncword = show_bits_long(&s->gb, 32);
 
-        if (extradata_syncword == DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_1 || extradata_syncword == DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_2) {
+        if (extradata_syncword == DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_1 || extradata_syncword == DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_2 ||
+            extradata_syncword == DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_3 || extradata_syncword == DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_4 ||
+            extradata_syncword == DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_5) {
             s->x_syncword_present = 1;
+            /* All but 0x02000850 are the head of an object metadata element,
+             * and only those carry an object count in their last byte. */
+            if (extradata_syncword != DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_1)
+                s->x_object_syncword = extradata_syncword;
         } else if (extradata_syncword == DCA_SYNCWORD_XLL_X_IMAX) {
             s->x_imax_syncword_present = 1;
+            s->x_object_syncword = extradata_syncword;
+        }
+
+        /* That element's payload runs from its syncword to the end of the
+         * frame, and names the channel sets it carries. A frame whose byte
+         * cannot be read keeps what an earlier one said. */
+        if (s->x_object_syncword && s->x_object_syncword == extradata_syncword) {
+            int start = get_bits_count(&s->gb) >> 3;
+            int flags = ff_dca_x_height_flags(data + start, FFMIN(size, s->frame_size) - start);
+
+            if (flags)
+                s->x_height_flags = flags;
         }
     }
 
@@ -1464,6 +1482,45 @@ int ff_dca_xll_filter_frame(DCAXllDecoder *s, AVFrame *frame)
         avctx->profile = AV_PROFILE_DTS_HD_MA;
     }
 
+    /* How many objects the stream declared, reported in the level. The profile
+     * names the codec and nothing more; this says which variant of it, and a
+     * caller needs both.
+     *
+     * 0xF14000Dn is the first four bytes of the type-241 object metadata
+     * element, and the low nibble of that last byte is the element's
+     * declaration count minus one - so the nibble states how many objects the
+     * stream declares, less one. It is passed on as transmitted rather than as
+     * a count, because the field's job is to carry what the stream said and
+     * leave the reading of it to whoever asked.
+     *
+     * The level carries it because it is already there: nothing in the DCA
+     * decoder writes it, avcodec_parameters_from_context() already copies it
+     * beside the profile, and a caller already receives it. A profile per
+     * object count would instead mint a codec identity for what is a property
+     * of one, and every consumer that enumerates the DTS-HD MA family by hand
+     * would have to learn all of them or quietly mishandle the stream.
+     *
+     * The 0x02000850 form is no such element and was not recorded above, so it
+     * leaves the level at the AV_LEVEL_UNKNOWN it started at. That is how a
+     * stream which said nothing about objects is told from D0's declaration of
+     * one, and it is very nearly every DTS:X release in the wild.
+     *
+     * Its lifetime is the context's, deliberately: the syncword is kept once
+     * seen and survives a flush, exactly as the two present flags above it do.
+     * So the level and the profile always describe the same set of frames -
+     * neither goes stale before the other, and a seek does not empty the count
+     * while the profile still says DTS:X. The one cost is a context that reads
+     * D0 through D4 and then the older form, which keeps the last count it was
+     * given; the profile keeps saying DTS:X on the same terms.
+     *
+     * Above the nibble go the DCA_X_LEVEL_* flags, which say whether the
+     * element carries the height quartet. Only the element's payload says so:
+     * D0 is both 5.1 with a single object and no heights, and 7.1.4 with one.
+     * With DCA_X_LEVEL_SETS_READ clear, nothing was read and nothing is said.
+     * The flags live as long as the syncword, and for the same reasons. */
+    if (s->x_object_syncword)
+        avctx->level = (s->x_object_syncword & 0x0F) | s->x_height_flags;
+
     avctx->bits_per_raw_sample = p->storage_bit_res;
     avctx->bit_rate = 0;
 
diff --git a/libavcodec/dca_xll.h b/libavcodec/dca_xll.h
--- a/libavcodec/dca_xll.h
+++ b/libavcodec/dca_xll.h
@@ -135,6 +135,8 @@ typedef struct DCAXllDecoder {
 
     DCADSPContext   *dcadsp;
 
+    unsigned int x_object_syncword;   ///< The object metadata element's syncword, when one was seen
+    int    x_height_flags;            ///< DCA_X_LEVEL_* read from that element's payload, 0 until read
     int    x_syncword_present;        ///< Syncword for extension data at end of frame (DTS:X) is present
     int    x_imax_syncword_present;   ///< Syncword for extension data at end of frame (DTS:X IMAX) is present
 
